# knobs_9x9.env -- mission ktg-train, node arxiv-1902.10565::derive_cycle_knobs_9x9.
# The production cycle knobs, and the single source of truth for them.
# Obligations o24_cycle_knobs_derived and the knob conjunct of o13_loop_config_paths.
#
# Sourceable: `set -a; . knobs_9x9.env; set +a` exports every knob into the
# ${VAR:-default} block of codes/loop/synchronous_loop_9x9.sh (CHANGE 9), whose
# defaults are identical to the values here (asserted by
# `codes/eval/derive_knobs.py --assert-loop-defaults`).
#
# NOTHING HERE IS HAND-PICKED (DESIGN section 8 R14). Every value is the solution of a
# constraint read out of the reference code at path:line, evaluated at a number
# measured by Slurm jobs 298712 and 299259 and read from the job-suffixed evidence
# copies evidence/smoke/{rows_per_game,throughput_smoke,nlwp_max,audit}-298712/299259.*
# -- never from the unsuffixed names, which attempt 2 overwrote (obligation o37).
# Re-derive with:
#   python3 codes/eval/derive_knobs.py \
#     --rows-per-game "$(cat evidence/smoke/rows_per_game-298712.txt)" \
#     --reuse 8 --samples-per-epoch 20000 --games 1000 --keep 120000 --cap 100000 \
#     --min-rows 25000 --taper 50000 --batch 128 --cpus 32 --game-threads 18 \
#     --gate-games-measured 156
# and re-check against the measured JSON with codes/eval/check_knobs_9x9.py.
#
# --gate-games-measured is needed only for the two -298712 / -299259 files, which were
# written before audit_smoke.py emitted `gatekeeper_games_total` (156 is that job's
# measured gatekeeper sgf line count, audit-298712.json S4_gatekeeper_sgfs.lines, and
# check_knobs_9x9.py reads it from there rather than taking it as an argument). Obligation
# o41: derive_knobs.py has no fallback for any measured key -- a missing one is an exit
# naming the key, never a constant.
#
# MEASURED INPUTS -- jobs 298712 (attempt 1) and 299259 (attempt 2, ppid-filtered sampler,
# no foreign pids). Cited by name: evidence/smoke/audit-298712.json and audit-299259.json;
# sha256 of every file read is in evidence/derive_cycle_knobs/derivation.md section 1.
#   r      = 32.3    rows/game, REAL net   (646 rows / 20 games, 298712 leg-D1 probe)
#                    corroborated 31.95 (639/20) and 34.4 (2064/60) by 299259's two probes;
#                    all three re-run in derivation.txt block 4 and none moves a knob
#   r_lo   = 25.0775 = r * (1 - 1/sqrt(20)), the section 11 lower bound used for every
#            binding inequality, so a real r below the 20-game measurement still holds
#   r0     = 31.675  rows/game, random net (2534 rows / 80 games)
#   r0_lo  = 28.1336 = r0 * (1 - 1/sqrt(80))
#   353.8  bytes/row on disk (896536 B / 2534 rows), i.e. 10.94 KiB/game at r0 and
#          11.16 KiB/game at r -- both above the 10 KiB/game c10 second conjunct, which
#          299259 refuted; the guard arithmetic below uses B/row, never KiB/game
#   2031.7 real-net selfplay games/h (299259, 80 real-net games / 141.75 s); 298712's
#          20-game probe gave 2341.5, and check_knobs_9x9.py takes the SLOWER of the two
#   14.243 train samples/s all-in (298712; torch.compile included); 299259 gave 17.202
#          and the check again takes the slower
#   threads: 22 random-net selfplay, 25 any CUDA-context stage, 14 train, 4+8 shuffle --
#          298712 and 299259 agree, and 299259's sampler had no foreign pids at all
#
# NOT USED, deliberately: nothing here is derived from cheapSearchProb or from assumption
# a07_moves_per_game_80. 299259 measured full_frac = 0.342 +- 0.006 over three runs against
# the 1 - cheapSearchProb = 0.25 the derivation.md section 4 arithmetic predicts, so the
# "(1-0.75)*80 + 0.02*80 ~ 22 rows/game" chain is refuted. Every row count below is the
# measured rows/game, which is what makes this derivation independent of that failure.

# --- the eleven loop-block knobs -------------------------------------------------

# G >= max(1.2*E/r_lo, 1.25*E/r0_lo) = max(957.0, 888.6), rounded up.
# First term: freshness -- a cycle must yield at least 20 % more NEW rows than one epoch
# draws, so the trainer never re-runs a window it has exhausted. Second term: the cycle-1
# bootstrap must reach SHUFFLE_MINROWS (shuffle.py:1090 exits 0 below it; :1077 caps the
# usable random rows at min_rows). Upstream :57 = 500, which fails BOTH at the measured r.
NUM_GAMES_PER_CYCLE=1000

# Upstream :58, unchanged. Measured 4-thread parent + 8 single-thread workers = 12 threads.
NUM_THREADS_FOR_SHUFFLING=8

# E/batch = 156 >= 100 = print_train_loss_every_batches (train.py:1379), so
# metrics_train.json is written at all -- at the smoke's 38 batches/epoch it stayed 0 bytes.
# The floor is 100*128 = 12800; 20000 is that rounded up to a round number, and is also
# DESIGN section 2's pilot value. Upstream :59 = 100000 (a 19x19 number).
NUM_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_EPOCH=20000

# Upstream :60, the hard reuse cap; never raised (task file section 13). The set actually
# uses 3.10 (lower90 3.99), so a 2x error in r still cannot cross 8.
MAX_TRAIN_PER_DATA=8

# = NUM_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_EPOCH // 2, which is train.py:441's own default made explicit.
NUM_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_SWA=10000

# Upstream :62, unchanged. Peak VRAM was 4094 MiB at batch 32 on a 180 GB B200, so 128
# has ~40x headroom; and 156 batches/epoch is what the metrics floor above needs.
BATCHSIZE=128

# = 1.25 * E. In cycle 1 the shuffle window IS min_rows (shuffle.py:1077 caps the usable
# random rows there, and :414-435 then returns exactly min_rows), so min_rows alone decides
# whether cycle 1 can fill an epoch: train.py:1303-1346 returns None -- and -quit-if-no-data
# exits 0 with NO export (:1487-1488) -- below round(E/batch) batches. 25000 rows = 195
# batches against the 156 an epoch needs. Reachable: 1000 * r0_lo = 28134 >= 25000.
# Upstream :63 = 100000.
SHUFFLE_MINROWS=25000

# = 5 * E, keeping upstream's own cap/epoch ratio of 5 (:64 500000 / :59 100000). This knob
# is what fixes EPOCHS_PER_EXPORT, because train.py:1257 caps the bucket at
# max(MAX_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_CYCLE, E) and :1434 spends E per epoch. The ratio survives the
# measurement: 5*E = 100000 <= (MAX_TRAIN_PER_DATA/2) * G * r_lo = 100310, i.e. even at half
# the reuse cap and at the conservative r the cycle's own new data pays for all five epochs.
MAX_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_CYCLE=100000

# Upstream :65, unchanged: no measured constraint binds it. It only sets where the shuffle
# window's growth turns sublinear (shuffle.py:421); at 9x9 scale the window is still in its
# near-linear regime (window ~ min_rows + 0.4 * post-random rows) for the first ~20 cycles.
TAPER_WINDOW_SCALE=50000

# = 1.2 * MAX_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_CYCLE, keeping upstream's keep/cap ratio (:66 600000 / :64
# 500000). Satisfies the shipped rule keep > cap (:66) and holds a whole cycle's draw
# (5*E = 100000 rows) with 20 % margin. Upstream :66 = 600000.
SHUFFLE_KEEPROWS=120000

# = floor(min(gain, cap_eff) / E) with gain = G*r*MAX_TRAIN_PER_DATA = 258400 (lower90
# 200620) and cap_eff = max(cap, E) = 100000 -> 5, the same at r and at r_lo.
# Passed to train.py as BOTH -epochs-per-export and -max-epochs-this-instance, so the
# persistent export_cycle_counter (train.py:871,975,1743,1831) can advance by at most 5 per
# cycle: never more than ONE candidate per cycle. Exactly one per cycle from cycle 13 on,
# when the shuffle window first holds 5*E rows; before that the counter carries across
# cycles and the export slips, which is upstream's own designed behaviour.
EPOCHS_PER_EXPORT=5

# --- the two allocation-side knobs, coupled to the ones above --------------------
# These are NOT read by synchronous_loop_9x9.sh. They are the numbers that
# codes/loop/loop.sbatch (#SBATCH --cpus-per-task, REQ_CPUS) and codes/cfg/{selfplay,
# gatekeeper}_9x9.cfg (numGameThreads) must carry; wiring them is o38, owned by
# loop_resume_under_walltime / cfg_9x9_override, not by this node.
#
# DESIGN section 1's [BLOCKING]: a 24-CPU declaration is false for any stage holding a CUDA
# context -- job 298712 measured nlwp_max 25 on both real-net stages against the 22 that
# every random-net selfplay measured, the +3 being CUDA runtime threads that
# debugSkipNeuralNet never creates (setup.cpp:126). Two repairs were admissible; with the
# CPU cap withdrawn (mission.json decisions[0]) raising the declaration is free, while
# cutting numGameThreads would lower the NN queue depth that DESIGN section 1 already calls
# the reason the GPU sits at 21.5 % mean duty. So the game threads stay and the declaration
# rises.
#
# Thread budget at numGameThreads 18, worst stage first:
#   gatekeeper, two real nets  18 + 2 nnServer + 1 dataWrite + 1 main + 2x3 CUDA = 29
#   selfplay, real net + switch 18 + 1 nnServer + 1 dataWrite + 1 modelLoad + 1 main
#                               + 3 CUDA = 25 (measured), + 2 net-switch transient = 27
#   train                       14 (measured, OMP_NUM_THREADS=MKL_NUM_THREADS=4)
#   shuffle                     4-thread parent + 8 worker processes = 12 (measured)
# max = 29 <= 32, headroom 3. One node has 124 CPUs and there is no CPU cap; the rule that
# survives is only "declared >= used".
KTG_CPUS_PER_TASK=32
KTG_NUM_GAME_THREADS=18

# --- projections that come with this set (not knobs; recorded so they can be refuted) ---
# cycle wall  = 3544 s selfplay (1000 games at a 0.5x-derated 2031.7 games/h)
#             + 7021 s train (5*20000 samples at 14.243 samples/s)
#             +  180 s gate + 377 s shuffle + 60 s export = 11182 s = 3.11 h
#             -> 23 whole cycles inside one 2-23:30:00 chain link. [PRELIMINARY]
# storage     = 16.1 MiB/cycle monotonic (tdata 10.9 + sgfs 2.3 + one export 2.9)
#             + 553 MiB bounded steady state (3 shuffle dirs, 10 rejected models,
#               10 checkpoints, 3 dated archives)
#             -> 20.90 GiB after a full 23-cycle link against the 500 GiB cap
#                (KTG_SCRATCH_HARD_BYTES in codes/data_budget/budget.env); 30480 cycles
#                would fit before the cap.
#
# [OPEN] real-net games/hour rests on 100 probe games total (298712: 20 in 30.75 s;
#        299259: 20 in 44.41 s and 60 in 96.24 s), all with logSearchInfo writing a
#        multi-MB log and none inside a real production cycle. The 0.5x derate above is a
#        planning allowance, not a measurement; node measure_stage_throughput owns it.
# [OPEN] KTG_CPUS_PER_TASK 32 clears the coordinator floor of >= 25 (28 with the
#        net-switch allowance) with 4 more for the still-unmeasured two-real-net gate.
# [OPEN] the two-real-net gatekeeper's thread count is unmeasured; 29 is arithmetic on the
#        one-net measurement of 25. First measurable at gatekeeper_stage, cycle 3 or later.
# [OPEN] the loop copy and the two cfgs still declare 24 CPUs; o38 wires KTG_CPUS_PER_TASK.
